# gtools/mkgolf2.golfj — `mkblob2.build_golf2()` in GOLF (M-TOOL, wave 8).
#
# The capstone of gtools/: the stage that produces self/golf2.golf, the
# generated source of the compiler this program was compiled by.  Everything it
# does is one splice — take the code-page encoded self/golf2.golfj, find the
# single `@BLOB@` marker, put the blob escape there instead — but that splice is
# the last piece of the build that was still only expressible in Python.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE PIPELINE.  This tool is stage three of three, and deliberately no more.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#     gtools/encode < self/golf2.golfj | gtools/mkgolf2 BLOB-ESCAPE-FILE \
#                                                       > self/golf2.golf
#
# where BLOB-ESCAPE-FILE holds what `gtools/mkblob` writes: the raw escape
# `` ` `` + the decimal byte count + one space + the blob.  Encoding and blob
# assembly are NOT repeated here.  `mkblob2.build_golf2()` is a Python function
# and can just call `codepage.encode` and `blob_escape()`; a GOLF tool is a
# process, so composition is spelled with a pipe and an argument instead — but
# it is the same composition, and duplicating either of those two tools inside
# this one would create exactly the second copy that the whole shadow
# architecture exists to prevent.
#
# Each stage is separately differential-tested against its Python counterpart in
# test/gtools.sh, so the pipeline is trustworthy joint by joint rather than only
# end to end.
#
# EITHER IMPLEMENTATION MAY STAND IN — that is the point of the shadow (see
# gtools/README.md).  The Python originals produce byte-identical input, so this
# tool is developed and tested against them and the mixed pipeline is a legal
# way to run it:
#
#     python3 tools/codepage.py encode < self/golf2.golfj \
#       | build/gmkgolf2 <(python3 -c 'import sys; sys.path.insert(0,"tools");
#                                      import mkblob2
#                                      sys.stdout.buffer.write(
#                                          mkblob2.blob_escape())')
#
# That is how it was written, since gtools/encode.golfj and gtools/mkblob.golfj
# were being authored in parallel with this file and did not exist yet.  Nothing
# below knows or cares which side produced its input.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE SPLICE RULES — from `mkblob2.build_golf2()`, which stays the source of
# truth.  Read it, not this summary.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   * The marker is the six ASCII bytes @BLOB@, and it must occur EXACTLY ONCE.
#     Zero or more than one is fatal: Python raises SystemExit naming the count,
#     and so does this tool — a message on fd 2 that ends in the count, and
#     exit(1).  The check is meaningful precisely because the encoder has
#     already stripped comments, so an `@BLOB@` written inside a comment (there
#     is one, in self/golf2.golfj's own header) never reaches this stage.
#   * The splice happens AFTER encoding, on the RAW BYTE STREAM.  The blob is
#     arbitrary bytes; a `#` inside it would make the encoder eat the rest of a
#     "line", and a 0x8E would open a string literal.  So this tool is byte
#     plumbing and nothing else: it never looks at what it is copying.
#   * Everything outside the marker passes through untouched.  The output
#     contains NUL bytes and machine code, so nothing here may treat 0 as a
#     terminator: every range is an explicit (address, length) pair — the
#     lib/ttext.golfj currency — and every byte goes out through lib/tio.golfj's
#     buffered `g`, which is a byte sink and has no opinion about the value.
#
# Matches are counted NON-OVERLAPPING (the scan resumes six bytes past a hit),
# which is what Python's `bytes.count` does, so `@BLOB@BLOB@` is one match in
# both.  Nothing in the repo contains that, but the two implementations agreeing
# on a case neither will ever see costs one word of arithmetic.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Words: B and C — the entire remaining uppercase pool (lib/prelude.golfj's
# header: "Free: B and C").  a-z are spoken for by the three tool libraries.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   B  addr len ->   emit a byte range through `g`
#   C  str ->        write a “…“ block to fd 2 (no newline, no exit)
#
# `B` is the whole output path: the result is three ranges — the source before
# the marker, the blob escape, the source after the marker — and a word that
# emits one range makes the splice three lines with no branches in them.  It
# uses the prelude's s0/s1 under an X/Y frame rather than variables, so it
# cannot alias anything the main program holds; `g` itself touches no scratch,
# which is what makes the loop body a single call.
#
# `C` exists because lib/tio.golfj's `a` (die) is atomic — message, newline,
# exit — and the error message here has to be interrupted by a NUMBER that is
# only known at run time.  So the fixed part goes out through `C`, the count is
# formatted with ttext's `o`, and the newline and exit(1) are written inline
# rather than borrowing `a` for a message it would have to be given empty.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Variables.  The bank at 0x4E0000 is a SEPARATE NAMESPACE from word names —
# `→p` names bank cell 'p', not lib/ttext.golfj's word `p` — so a tool program
# may use any letter for a variable.  Reserved: `s`, which lib/tutf.golfj holds
# its state block in (../REGISTRY.md §2.1).  This program never spells →s / ←s.
#
#   p n   the encoded source: address and length (slurped from fd 0)
#   q u   the blob escape:    address and length (slurped from argv[1])
#   i     scan cursor — how far into the source the marker search has got
#   w     the index `r` just returned, relative to the cursor
#   k     how many markers have been seen
#   x     the offset of the FIRST one — the splice point
#   z     the scan's stop flag: 0 keep going, 1 no more matches
#   v y   the decimal-formatting buffer and the digit count (error path only)
#
# THE -1 NEVER REACHES A VARIABLE (../REGISTRY.md §2.1).  `r` answers -1 for
# "not found", and a -1 in a bank cell is the classic way to lose an afternoon:
# when this was written a cell was four bytes and `←x` zero-extended, so a
# stored -1 came back as 4294967295, every signed test on it answered "not
# negative", and a loop bound derived from one ran about four billion times.
# (W8A/M3W has since widened the bank to eight bytes and made `←x` a full 64-bit
# load, which retires that particular trap.)  The discipline below is kept
# either way, because it costs nothing and is right under both: the -1 is tested
# with the signed `≺` WHILE IT IS STILL ON THE DATA STACK, and what reaches
# `→w` is either a real index or a 0 the stopped scan will not look at.  No
# variable here ever holds a negative value.
#
# The argv pointer is likewise never stored — it is a STACK address, above 2^32
# on Linux x86-64, and it goes from `h` straight into `b` on the data stack.
# `p` and `q` are heap addresses (the prelude's brk heap) and are safe in a
# variable under either bank width.
#
# NOTHING HERE IS SIZED.  The marker is six bytes and that is the only constant
# in the program: the source length, the blob length and the splice point are
# all read from the input.  A blob that grows an atom, or a self/golf2.golfj
# that grows a page, changes no number below.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# House rules, inherited from lib/prelude.golfj and the tool libraries.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   * Raw atoms only for address, index and flag arithmetic: \radd \rsub, never
#     the ASCII + - that M-VEC made polymorphic.
#   * `{…}` is a DO-while.  The scan loop is deliberately UNGUARDED: it must run
#     once even on empty input, because running `r` once is how "no marker at
#     all" is discovered — and `r` with a needle longer than the haystack
#     answers -1 without touching memory, so a 0-byte source is safe.  `B`'s
#     loop IS guarded, since two of its three ranges can legitimately be empty
#     (a marker at offset 0, or one at the very end of the file).
#   * `[…]` runs on TOS == 0, `}` loops on 0 — opposite polarities, so the stop
#     flag is read once for each.
#   * Byte 0x5C (swap) never appears: `decode` would render it as a bare `\`
#     that `encode` would then read as a mnemonic escape.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# B  addr len ->  emit the range through tio's buffered `g`.  s0 walks the
# address, s1 counts down the remaining bytes.  Guarded on len, so an empty
# range emits nothing.  `g` uses no scratch, so the body is one call deep and
# the frame is only needed because s0/s1 must survive it.
#
# `g` AND NOT `d`, deliberately.  `1 addr len d` would write the same bytes in
# one syscall instead of one per 8192, and it is tempting because all three
# ranges are already contiguous.  But `d` is the raw primitive `f` is built out
# of, not the stream: a tool that wrote its artifact with `d` on fd 1 while
# anything else in the process emitted through `g` would interleave the two out
# of order, and `g` is the stream every other tool under gtools/ produces its
# output on.  One stream per tool (lib/tio.golfj's header) is worth more than
# three syscalls, and the buffer makes the cost of the byte loop a rounding
# error next to the read that produced the bytes.
:B X5177448\store 5177440\store 5177448\fetch 0\eq[{5177440\fetch? g 5177440\fetch 1\radd 5177440\store 5177448\fetch 1\rsub 5177448\store 5177448\fetch 0\eq}]Y;

# C  str ->  write a “…“ block to fd 2, unterminated.  A block is [len:4][bytes]
# and its value is the address of the length field, so the payload is addr+4 and
# the count is a 32-bit `@` — the one deliberate 4-byte read in this file, and
# the same one lib/tio.golfj's `a` and the prelude's `O` do.
:C X5177440\store 2 5177440\fetch 4\radd 5177440\fetch@ d Y;

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The program.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# argv[1] is the blob-escape file.  `h` answers 0 when the argument is not
# there, which is the only spelling that cannot read past the end of argv.  The
# pointer stays on the data stack across the test and into `b` — see the
# 4-byte-bank note above.
1 h "0\eq 1\radd[“usage: mkgolf2 BLOB-ESCAPE-FILE  (encoded source on stdin, golf2.golf on stdout)“a]
0 b e \setu\setq

# The encoded source arrives on stdin.  `e` loops over short reads, so a pipe
# from gtools/encode is read to EOF exactly like a file.
0 e \setn\setp

# Count the markers, and remember where the first one is.  `r` searches the
# REMAINING source each time; the cursor jumps six bytes past a hit so matches
# do not overlap.  `r`'s -1 is turned into a stop flag on the data stack and
# never stored.
0\setk 0\setx 0\seti 0\setz
{\getp\geti\radd \getn\geti\rsub “@BLOB@“4\radd 6 r
 "0\slt 1\radd[_ 1\setz 0]\setw
 \getz[\getk[\geti\getw\radd\setx] \getk 1\radd\setk \geti\getw\radd 6\radd\seti]
 \getz}

# Exactly one, or nothing is written.  `k-1 ≡ 0` is -1 when the count is right,
# so `[` runs the failure arm and only the failure arm; it exits and never
# returns, which is why the splice below needs no else.
\getk 1\rsub 0\eq[“mkgolf2: expected exactly one @BLOB@ marker on stdin, found “C
 4 A\setv \getk\getv o\sety 10 \getv\gety\radd, 2 \getv\gety 1\radd d 1 0 0 60\sys]

# The splice: source before the marker, the blob escape, source after it.
\getp\getx B
\getq\getu B
\getp\getx\radd 6\radd \getn\getx\rsub 6\rsub B
f
